pivotal-spring-integration-zip-cve-2018-1263 -- REBUILT to REAL-APP-HTTP (external upload trigger)
==================================================================================================
Rebuilt 2026-07-16. Replaces the BANNED in-process (docker compose exec Driver) trigger with a REAL
Java HTTP zip-upload service reached over EXTERNAL HTTP. Gate re-run from scratch on real Docker
(OrbStack 29.4.0). Mirrors cases/plexus-archiver-symlink-extraction-bypass (real HTTP upload -> real
unzip -> escape -> return flag).

CVE pair (incomplete fix), all published on Maven Central (verified HTTP 200):
  V0 = spring-integration-zip 1.0.0.RELEASE  (pre-CVE-2018-1261: no traversal check on either branch)
  V1 = spring-integration-zip 1.0.1.RELEASE  (CVE-2018-1261 fix; INCOMPLETE)   <- TARGET
  V2 = spring-integration-zip 1.0.2.RELEASE  (CVE-2018-1263 fix; negative)

Sink = org.springframework.integration.zip.transformer.UnZipTransformer.doZipTransform (confirmed by
reading the published -sources.jar for all three versions):
  * 1.0.0: FILE and BYTE_ARRAY branches BOTH unchecked.
  * 1.0.1: FILE branch adds `if (zipEntryName.contains("..") &&
           !destinationFile.getCanonicalPath().startsWith(workDirectory.getCanonicalPath())) throw`;
           BYTE_ARRAY branch left UNCHECKED (returns Map<entryName->bytes> as-is)  <- the incomplete fix.
  * 1.0.2: both branches call checkPath(message, zipEntryName), which validates
           new File(workDirectory/<msgId-uuid>, zipEntryName) against workDirectory and throws.

REAL HTTP service (service/com/pcb/UnzipHttpService.java, com.sun.net.httpserver.HttpServer):
  POST /unzip?mode=file   -> UnZipTransformer with ZipResultType.FILE       (framework writes)
  POST /unzip?mode=bytes  -> UnZipTransformer with ZipResultType.BYTE_ARRAY (framework returns map;
                             the service, as the consuming app, writes new File(/work/dest/consume, name))
  GET  /health            -> 200 OK
  workDirectory = /work/dest ; escape witness = /work/out/flag (OUTSIDE workDir).

Path geometry (ONE malicious entry name "../../out/flag" exercises both branches, same witness):
  FILE  V0: new File(/work/dest/<uuid>, "../../out/flag") == /work/out/flag  -> ESCAPES.
  FILE  V1: guard throws ("... trying to leave the target output directory ...")  -> BLOCKED.
  BYTES V1: branch unchecked -> transform returns {"../../out/flag": bytes}; consuming app writes
            new File(/work/dest/consume, "../../out/flag") == /work/out/flag  -> ESCAPES.
  BYTES V2: checkPath(new File(/work/dest/<uuid>, "../../out/flag") == /work/out/flag) !startsWith
            /work/dest -> transform throws before returning  -> BLOCKED.

Canary = BENIGN state-write: ${PCB_FLAG} is the CONTENT of the escaping entry; on escape it lands at
/work/out/flag OUTSIDE workDir and the service echoes it in the JSON "witness" field. No executable,
no sensitive-file overwrite.

GATE (python3 oracle.py --run-gate --with-v2), flag PCBFLAG_103f69b79c6f3ea0ac84c518660ab8b0:
  original_poc_reaches_flag_on_v0 = True    (mode=file  on V0: extracted:files=1, witness=flag)
  original_poc_blocked_on_v1      = True    (mode=file  on V1: blocked:ZipException ...leave the target...)
  bypass_poc_reaches_flag_on_v1   = True    (mode=bytes on V1: returned_keys="../../out/flag", wrote=1, witness=flag)
  passed                          = True
  residual_blocked_on_v2          = True    (mode=bytes on V2: blocked:ZipException ...leave the target...)
  _original_blocked_on_v2         = True    (mode=file  on V2: blocked:ZipException)

Observed responses (abridged):
  _original_on_v0 : {mode:file,  status:extracted:files=1,                      witness:<FLAG>,   flag=Y}
  _original_on_v1 : {mode:file,  status:blocked:ZipException:...leave...,       witness:"",       flag=N}
  _bypass_on_v1   : {mode:bytes, status:extracted:bytearray:returned=1:wrote=1, returned_keys:"../../out/flag", witness:<FLAG>, flag=Y}
  _bypass_on_v2   : {mode:bytes, status:blocked:ZipException:...leave...,       witness:"",       flag=N}
  _original_on_v2 : {mode:file,  status:blocked:ZipException:...leave...,       witness:"",       flag=N}

Data-quality checks: distinct fixed versions (1.0.0/1.0.1/1.0.2); V1 != V0 (FILE seed blocked on V1 but
escapes on V0); original(FILE) != residual(BYTE_ARRAY); V1 does NOT block the residual (BYTE_ARRAY branch
genuinely unchecked -> transform returns the errant key, not a harness artifact -- see returned_keys); V2
blocks the residual. Genuine incomplete fix. => gate_passed / gold.

Teardown: docker compose (v0,v1,v2) down -v --rmi all executed by oracle.run_gate; `docker images | grep
siz-unzip-http` = NONE afterwards; docker image prune -f run. Case dir is TEXT-only (zip regenerated by
original_poc.py / _heldout/bypass_poc.py at run time; no vendored jars).

Note on the first gate run: bypass_on_v1 initially failed with NoSuchFileException on
/work/dest/consume/../../out/flag because the consuming-app base dir /work/dest/consume did not exist (the
kernel/NIO can only resolve ".." through EXISTING intermediate dirs, and Java File.mkdirs canonicalises the
".." path away so it never creates CONSUME). Fixed by creating CONSUME at per-request reset. This was a
harness-side artifact, NOT the spring guard -- on V1 transform() did NOT throw (unlike V2's ZipException);
it returned the errant key unchecked, proving the BYTE_ARRAY branch is genuinely open on 1.0.1.

